# The forms of a fence

## Backticks and tildes

```python#eval
x = 1
```

~~~python#eval
x = 2
~~~

## A longer fence holds a shorter one

````python#eval id=outer
text = """
```
not the end of the cell
```
"""
````

## Attributes

A value and a bare word, then a quoted value. HQL reads `id` and `hidden`
and no other attribute, so the quoted one is on a Python cell.

```hql#eval id=a hidden
cards | size
```

```python#eval id=b title="Two words" timeout=30 cache
print("attributes")
```

## Upper case

```Python#eval
x = 3
```

```HQL
cards | size
```

## Indented by up to three spaces

   ```hql#eval
   cards | size
   ```

## No blank line between a paragraph and a fence
```hql
cards | size
```
The paragraph continues here, and `[[links]]` still reads as a link: [[links]].

## Forms that stay with the Markdown grammar

A colon, a comma or a brace after the name is a form of the Markdown grammar,
which reads these two fences.

```python:example.py
x = 4
```

```{.python}
x = 5
```

## Fences the extension does not reach yet

A fence inside a list item or a block quote is read by the Markdown grammar.
A plain `python` fence is coloured there. A cell is not, and an `hql` fence is
not.

- A plain fence in a list:

  ```python
  x = 6
  ```

- A cell in a list:

  ```python#eval
  x = 7
  ```

- An HQL fence in a list:

  ```hql
  cards | size
  ```

> A cell in a quote:
>
> ```hql#eval
> cards | size
> ```
